+  g_match: {t: 'Same card / Not the same card, and the match labels', f: 'your answer for the WHOLE card (every grade), and the two checks that ask for it', m: 'Same card: confirms the match for every grade; both labels go away. Not the same card: for every grade - its rows are hidden from the table, nothing is deleted, the “Not the same” button shows them again (greyed) so an answer can be changed, and the next Card Ladder run looks for the card’s real page. Withdraw my answer takes it back. unsure match (amber): the automatic matcher was not sure - its status “review” only means a person should check it; nothing is sent anywhere. It stays until you answer. likely wrong match (red): the price check - for every month and grade both sites priced the card (CGC 10 Pristine / Perfect left out), Card Ladder’s monthly average is divided by PriceCharting’s; when the typical gap is 2× or more and at least 80% of 6 or more months are more than 1.5× apart, the match is probably a different card. One odd month does not count, and fewer than 6 months is too few to judge (low supply moves prices a lot). It never hides the unsure label. It learns from your answers: every Same / Not the same you give becomes a tested example (kept with the check’s numbers in card-data/data/ids/match_checks.csv). After about 20-30 answers, including a few wrong matches (at least 3 of each kind), the site build fits the thresholds to them - it picks the gap and share that best separate your answers, and the hover says so. Until then they stay fixed and conservative.', e: 'Charizard #4 Celebrations: Card Ladder’s page is the Ultra-Premium Collection metal Charizard; its PSA 10 sold for about 14× PriceCharting’s in 55 of 55 months - likely wrong match - answered Not the same card.'},
+  g_grade: {t: 'PC Grade', f: 'the grade PriceCharting’s figures on this row are for, from PriceCharting itself', m: 'One row per card per grade (which grades show is set in the Grading button). PSA 9 = PriceCharting recorded sales of PSA 9 slabs, or names the grade itself (its PSA 10 line, its population report). PriceCharting’s Grade 1 - 9.5 prices blend every grader (PSA, CGC, BGS, SGC …), so they are never used as a PSA or CGC price. none = PriceCharting has nothing of this grade and grader of its own. RED = PC Grade and CL Grade are not the same grade (the PC to CL % then says ≠ grade). “Raw” marks an ungraded card in the raw + graded view. Labels: amber “unsure match” = the automatic matcher was not sure Card Ladder’s card is PriceCharting’s card (its status “review” only means a person should check it; nothing is sent anywhere); red “likely wrong match” = Card Ladder’s price has sat far from PriceCharting’s for most of the months both priced the card (hover for the numbers). Answer them in Review (the ? there explains both).', e: 'PSA 10; CGC 9.5; none.'},
   g_clgrade: {t: 'CL Grade', f: 'the grade Card Ladder’s figures on this row are for, from Card Ladder itself', m: 'Card Ladder’s own label for the grade on the card’s page (PSA 10, CGC 10 Pristine …), or a grade it recorded sales of. none = Card Ladder has no such grade for the card, so its columns on this row stay empty. The CL prices, sales and picture on a row are always Card Ladder’s own of this grade. RED = not the same grade as PC Grade (prices not compared).', e: 'CGC 10 Pristine.'},
